Question 6 – Maximum Number of Distinct Elements in a Sliding Window
Problem
Given an array and a window size K, find the maximum number of distinct elements present in any contiguous window of size K.
Solution – Java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] arr = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            arr[i] = sc.nextInt();

        int k = sc.nextInt();

        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++)
            freq.put(arr[i], freq.getOrDefault(arr[i], 0) + 1);

        int maxDistinct = freq.size();

        for (int i = k; i < n; i++) {

            int outgoing = arr[i - k];

            freq.put(outgoing, freq.get(outgoing) - 1);

            if (freq.get(outgoing) == 0)
                freq.remove(outgoing);

            int incoming = arr[i];

            freq.put(incoming,
                    freq.getOrDefault(incoming, 0) + 1);

            maxDistinct = Math.max(maxDistinct, freq.size());
        }

        System.out.println(maxDistinct);
    }
}
Optimization
Do not recreate a HashSet for every window.
Instead, update the existing frequency map by:
1.	Removing the outgoing element.
2.	Adding the incoming element.
3.	Checking map.size().

Sample Input
8
1 2 1 3 4 3 2 1
4
Sample Output
4
Explanation
Windows:
1 2 1 3 → 3 distinct
2 1 3 4 → 4 distinct
1 3 4 3 → 3 distinct
3 4 3 2 → 3 distinct
4 3 2 1 → 4 distinct
Maximum = 4.
This is an important example of efficient collection manipulation using a sliding-window technique.
Question 7 – Smart Traffic Navigation
